:::
:::








@fig-cell_data_scatter_covariance_EDA-pdf is the static twin of the interactive figure in the web edition, where you can hover over each point.

:::

Pooled over all animals the two markers correlate moderately (r = 0.52), but look at the colors. The day-0 animals (dark purple) are packed near the origin and the day-3 animals just beyond them; the later days (green to yellow) spread across the plot. Within a single day the relationship is far from uniform: the correlation is 0.57 at day 0, 0.07 at day 3, 0.66 at day 7, 0.22 at day 14 and -0.08 at day 30. With ten animals per day these within-day correlations are rough, and I would not read much into a single one. But the pattern they make is the point: the straight line summarizes a relationship that is mostly *time moving both markers*, and it does not describe any one day well. Letting the line differ by day is what we call an interaction, and @sec-linear-regression-multiple fits the one the design allows.

## Two more shapes, borrowed: when the smoothing and the pooling matter

`cell_data` has taught us a lot, but two lessons are easier to see in other people's data, and a package I used in @sec-eda-missing-data, `NeuroDataSets` [@neurodatasets], has two small real datasets that make them vivid. I use them only in this section. The first is about how much a histogram or a density can *invent* or *hide*. The second is about what happens when a figure pools regions that behave differently, a cousin of pooling days.

















### Peaks that depend on the settings: spontaneous synaptic currents

Imagine you record from a neuron in a brain slice and, with no stimulation at all, collect the small currents that appear on their own. Each one is the response to neurotransmitter released from a presynaptic terminal. The old question behind this dataset [@paulsen1994] is whether release comes in units. If a synapse releases whole vesicles, amplitudes should come in multiples of one unit: many events of size *q*, fewer of size 2*q*, fewer of 3*q*. Think of buying eggs, which come by the half dozen and the dozen, never by the single yolk. A histogram of amplitudes should then show humps at regular spacing. `guineapig_neuro_df` holds the amplitudes of 346 spontaneous currents recorded in guinea-pig thalamic slices (the package labels the unit "pA or similar", and I leave the axes in the same words).

This is a dataset where a plain histogram can mislead in both directions, and I want you to see why. A histogram counts events in bins, so you choose the bin width; a density smooths the counts, so you choose the bandwidth. Too wide and a real hump vanishes; too narrow and noise looks like a hump. @fig-eda-ggplot-guineapig draws the same 346 numbers four ways with `geom_histogram()` and `geom_density()`, changing only that choice.








::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
# One neutral color for this dataset (not a marker color of this chapter)
gp_color <- "#E69F00"

# A function that draws a histogram of the amplitudes for a chosen bin width
gp_hist <- function(bw, title) {
  ggplot(gp, aes(x = y)) +
    geom_histogram(binwidth = bw, boundary = 0, fill = gp_color,
                   color = "white", alpha = 0.8) +
    labs(title = title, x = "Amplitude", y = "Count") +
    Plot_theme
}

# Three bin widths: too wide, a reasonable middle, too narrow
p_h1 <- gp_hist(4, "Bins of 4")
p_h2 <- gp_hist(1.5, "Bins of 1.5")
p_h3 <- gp_hist(0.3, "Bins of 0.3")

# Two smoothing levels of the density, drawn with different line types
p_d <- ggplot(gp, aes(x = y)) +
  geom_density(aes(linetype = "default smoothing"), fill = gp_color, alpha = 0.5) +
  geom_density(aes(linetype = "less smoothing"), adjust = 0.4) +
  scale_linetype_manual(name = NULL,
                        values = c("default smoothing" = "solid",
                                   "less smoothing" = "dashed")) +
  labs(title = "Density", x = "Amplitude", y = "Density") +
  Plot_theme + legend_top()

# Four panels, tagged A to D
(p_h1 | p_h2) / (p_h3 | p_d) + plot_annotation(tag_levels = "A")
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![Amplitude of spontaneous synaptic currents, drawn four ways. A to C: histograms with bins of 4, 1.5 and 0.3. D: densities with the default smoothing (solid) and with less smoothing (dashed).](eda-ggplot_files/figure-html/fig-eda-ggplot-guineapig-1.png){#fig-eda-ggplot-guineapig fig-alt='Four panels. A: a coarse histogram with a single hill and a long right tail. B: a medium histogram with a main peak near 7 to 8 and a smaller hump near 15. C: a very fine histogram that looks jagged, with many small spikes. D: two density curves, a smooth one with peaks near 7.5 and 15 and a wavier dashed one with extra small bumps.' width=960}
:::
:::








::: {.callout-tip title="How to read this figure"}
Compare the panels as if they were four photographs of the same animal taken with different lenses. Ask first what stays the same in all four (here, a big pile at low amplitudes and a long right tail). Then ask which features appear with only one setting. A feature that survives a sensible range of bins or bandwidths deserves attention; one that appears only at the narrowest setting is probably noise, and one that disappears only at the widest is probably just blurred. Never report a hump you found with one setting and could not find with a neighboring one.
:::

What does the dataset say? In panel A the data look like one hill with a tail. In panel B a second hump appears near 15.4, about twice the position of the main peak at 7.5, which is the spacing a quantal release would predict. Panel C shows what too little smoothing does: a jagged comb of narrow spikes that tempts you to count peaks that are mostly noise. In panel D the default density keeps the first two humps (at 7.5 and 15.4), and its third little bump at 28.8 stands on a single measurement (the largest, 28.8; only 1 current exceeds 26), so I do not count it. With less smoothing the dashed curve finds 7 peaks, and I cannot tell from 346 currents which of the extra ones are real. That is an honest EDA conclusion: the first two humps are supported, the rest is not, and a stronger claim needs another tool (a mixture model, for instance) and probably more recordings.

One more point about the summary. The mean is 9.8, the standard deviation 4.4 and the median 8.5. The mean sits between the first and the second hump, in a valley where few currents live, the same trap we met with GFAP at 30 DPI (@fig-eda-ggplot-raincloud). A mean and a standard deviation would tell a reader that the typical current is about 10, and almost no recorded current is typical in that sense.

### Pooling regions: GFAP and IBA1 in human brain tissue

The second dataset brings back our favorite marker in a different tissue. `aba_phenotype_data_df` comes from the Allen Brain Atlas study of aging, dementia and traumatic brain injury [@miller2017]. It has 377 brain tissue samples from four regions, each quantified by immunohistochemistry for GFAP (astrocytes, as in our mice) and IBA1 (microglia). The package gives only the acronyms, which I read as hippocampus (`HIP`), parietal and temporal neocortex (`PCx`, `TCx`) and forebrain white matter (`FWM`). The staining values have no documented unit, so I keep the axes named after the columns and compare only *between regions*, never against `cell_data`.

The question is the one we asked of time, now asked of region: does the shape of the distribution change from one region to the next? Think of four neighborhoods and their housing prices. A single histogram of the city hides that one neighborhood is cheap and tight and another expensive and spread out. With 91 to 99 samples per region we are well past the thirty-observation rule I gave for showing every point, but the rain is still useful, since it shows where the samples actually fall, and the half-density next to it keeps the shape visible. We use the same raincloud recipe as before, with the regions ordered by median GFAP, blue for GFAP as in the rest of the chapter and a new green for IBA1.








::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
Iba1_color <- "#009E73"

# GFAP by region: cloud, box, rain and mean
aba_gfap <- ggplot(aba, aes(x = region, y = ihc_gfap_ffpe)) +
  stat_halfeye(adjust = 0.5, width = 0.6, .width = 0,
               justification = -0.3, point_colour = NA) +
  geom_boxplot(width = 0.2, outlier.shape = NA) +
  geom_half_point(side = "l", range_scale = 0.4, alpha = 0.4) +
  stat_summary(fun = mean, geom = "point", shape = 18, size = 4, color = Gfap_color) +
  labs(x = "Brain region", y = "GFAP (ihc_gfap_ffpe)") +
  Plot_theme

# IBA1 by region, same layers
aba_iba1 <- ggplot(aba, aes(x = region, y = ihc_iba1_ffpe)) +
  stat_halfeye(adjust = 0.5, width = 0.6, .width = 0,
               justification = -0.3, point_colour = NA) +
  geom_boxplot(width = 0.2, outlier.shape = NA) +
  geom_half_point(side = "l", range_scale = 0.4, alpha = 0.4) +
  stat_summary(fun = mean, geom = "point", shape = 18, size = 4, color = Iba1_color) +
  labs(x = "Brain region", y = "IBA1 (ihc_iba1_ffpe)") +
  Plot_theme

aba_gfap + aba_iba1 + plot_annotation(tag_levels = "A")
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![GFAP and IBA1 immunohistochemistry by brain region in the Allen aging, dementia and TBI samples (raincloud plots; the diamond is the group mean)](eda-ggplot_files/figure-html/fig-eda-ggplot-aba-1.png){#fig-eda-ggplot-aba fig-alt='Two raincloud plots by region (TCx, PCx, HIP, FWM). A, GFAP, climbs from TCx to FWM, with the white matter highest and widest. B, IBA1, is highest and most spread out in the hippocampus, with the other three regions similar. Individual samples appear as points beside each cloud.' width=960}
:::
:::








@fig-eda-ggplot-aba says different things for the two markers. GFAP climbs from region to region: the median goes from 0.026 in TCx to 0.065 in FWM, and the white matter is also the widest. IBA1 is organized differently: the hippocampus stands out (median 0.075, against 0.048 to 0.057 in the other three regions). A single ranking of "regions by marker" would have been wrong for one of the two. This is the same lesson as the days of `cell_data`: the group is part of the answer, and a pooled figure answers a question nobody asked.

Two checks deserve a place in every EDA of this kind, because the figure alone would not tell you. First, ggplot2 drops missing values silently (we suppressed the warning above): 32 samples have no GFAP and 14 no IBA1, which leaves 345 complete rows out of 377, and whether those gaps are harmless is the question of @sec-eda-missing-data. Second, the table has one row per sample and no donor identifier, so I cannot tell which samples came from the same person. If several regions come from one brain, the rows are not independent, and a model that treats them as if they were commits the pseudoreplication that @sec-multilevel-varying-intercepts is about. A figure cannot warn you of that; you have to ask how the data were collected.

For one last look, the scatter of the two markers, split by region (@fig-eda-ggplot-aba-scatter). `facet_wrap()` gives each region its own panel on shared scales, the grown-up version of coloring by day in @fig-cell_data_scatter_covariance_EDA, and it needs no legend because the panel titles already say which region is which.








::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
# One panel per region, same axes, so the clouds can be compared
ggplot(aba, aes(x = ihc_iba1_ffpe, y = ihc_gfap_ffpe)) +
  geom_point(color = Gfap_color, alpha = 0.6) +
  geom_smooth(method = "lm", color = "black", se = FALSE, linewidth = 0.8) +
  facet_wrap(~ region) +
  labs(x = "IBA1 (ihc_iba1_ffpe)", y = "GFAP (ihc_gfap_ffpe)") +
  Plot_theme
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![GFAP against IBA1 in each brain region (one point per sample; the line is a straight-line fit)](eda-ggplot_files/figure-html/fig-eda-ggplot-aba-scatter-1.png){#fig-eda-ggplot-aba-scatter fig-alt='Four scatter plots, one per region (TCx, PCx, HIP, FWM), of GFAP against IBA1. The clouds are broad and sit at different heights in different regions. The straight lines are nearly flat in three regions and slope downward in FWM.' width=960}
:::
:::








In @fig-eda-ggplot-aba-scatter each region's cloud sits at its own height, and the lines are nearly flat except in `FWM`, where GFAP drifts down as IBA1 rises. Across all samples the correlation between the markers is 0.04; within the regions it is 0.13, -0.13, -0.04, -0.25 (in the order TCx, PCx, HIP, FWM). None of these is large (the strongest, in `FWM`, is -0.25), and with about 85 samples per region I would not lean on any single one. The lesson is the quieter one: a correlation computed on pooled groups mixes differences *between* groups with association *within* them. We saw the same in `cell_data`, where the days made the two markers move together, and it is why a coefficient on pooled data should be read only after looking at the groups.

## What we have learned about our data

Three questions, three kinds of figure, and one list of findings that will guide every model that follows. These are leads from looking, not results, and they are collected with the rest of the part's findings in @sec-eda-summary.

-   **Scale and shape.** PDGFR-β ranges from 47 to 1582 and GFAP from 25 to 1245, both right-skewed when pooled across days (@fig-cell_data_dens_EDA).
-   **Time trend.** Both markers rise with days post-ischemia, but not along a straight line: PDGFR-β keeps climbing, GFAP plateaus and dips at 30 DPI (@fig-cell_data_scatter_EDA, @fig-cell_data_rain_EDA).
-   **Unequal spread.** Early days (0 and 3 DPI) are tight, later days are widely scattered, which anticipates heteroskedasticity and means one common variance is a poor description.
-   **A summary that misleads.** At 30 DPI, GFAP has two groups of animals and a mean that falls in the gap between them (@fig-cell_data_rain_EDA).
-   **Covariation depends on the day.** The association between the two markers is moderate overall but varies by day, mostly because both markers share the effect of time (@fig-cell_data_scatter_covariance_EDA).

::: {.callout-note title="How to report it"}
**Methods.** Before modeling, we explored the raw data graphically (density plots, scatter plots against days post-ischemia, and raincloud plots showing every animal; n = 49, 7 to 12 per day). The standard deviation of GFAP integrated density rose from 25 at day 0 to 319 at day 7, and that of PDGFR-β integrated density from 68 at day 0 to 376 at day 30. Because the spread was not constant across days and the trend was not a straight line, we did not rely on a model with one common variance and a linear time trend without checking those assumptions explicitly.
:::

These findings fix the plan. We know that the modeling strategy must allow a trend that is not straight, a variance that changes with time, and a relationship between the markers that depends on the day. Two more tools complete the exploration before we start fitting: @sec-eda-gwalkr lets you explore the same data by dragging variables around, and @sec-eda-ggpairs screens all the pairs of variables at once.
